<question type="description"><name><text>Module 1 · Lesson 0 · Section 1: Introduction to the Script</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<h3>Section 1: Introduction to the Script</h3>
<p>Syriac is written from <strong>right to left</strong>. The script
used in this course is <strong>Estrangela</strong> (ܐܣܛܪܢܓܠܐ), meaning
“rounded.” It is the oldest and most formal of the three Syriac scripts,
and it is the primary script used in the earliest manuscripts of the
Peshitta and the Acts of the Martyrs.</p>
<p><strong>Core Principles:</strong></p>
<ol type="1">
<li><strong>22 Consonants</strong>: All letters are consonants. Vowels
are indicated by separate small marks or dots (to be covered in Lesson
1).</li>
<li><strong>Connecting Letters</strong>: Most letters connect to the
preceding and following letters (like cursive or Arabic). However, 8
letters are “non-joiners” — they only connect to the letter before
them.</li>
<li><strong>No Case</strong>: There are no capital letters.</li>
<li><strong>Alaph as Carrier</strong>: The first letter, Alaph (ܐ), is
often a silent carrier for a vowel or a glottal stop.</li>
</ol>
<hr />

]]></text></questiontext></question>
