<question type="description"><name><text>Module 1 · Lesson 0 · Section 3: Joiners vs. Non-Joiners</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<h3>Section 3: Joiners vs. Non-Joiners</h3>
<p>In your reading, you will notice that certain letters never connect
to the letter following them. This creates a small visual gap within a
word.</p>
<p><strong>The Non-Joiners (Letters that only connect to the
RIGHT):</strong></p>
<p>ܐ (Alaph), ܕ (Dalath), ܗ (He), ܘ (Waw), ܙ (Zain), ܨ (Sade), ܪ (Rish),
ܬ (Taw).</p>
<p><em>Note: Taw (ܬ) often appears to connect, but technically follows
the non-joiner rule in many formal manuscripts. Dalath (ܕ) and Rish (ܪ)
look identical except for a single dot: Dalath has a dot BELOW, Rish has
a dot ABOVE.</em></p>
<hr />

]]></text></questiontext></question>
